<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chengzhou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luoyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chengzhou, also called Luoyi, was the eastern capital of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, built near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> under the direction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> after the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-three-guards">Rebellion of the Three Guards</a>. It served as a base for controlling the former Shang lands and housed the Eight Armies. After the fall of the western capital in 771 BCE, King Ping moved the court there, beginning the Eastern Zhou.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chengzhou.jpg" alt="Chengzhou" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rialfver Original uploader was Rialfver at nl.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Longmen_Grottoes_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
